    sum: "Deliberately corrupted images — RGB splits, datamosh smears and static — as an emotional edit language.",
    desc: "Glitchcore is an edit-based aesthetic that applies visible digital corruption as emotional expression: RGB channel splits, datamoshing, pixel sorting, static bursts, scanlines and half-readable text layered over portraits, fandom art and symbols. While gallery Glitch Art dates to the 2000s as a formal interrogation of signal error, glitchcore is its louder, saturated, fandom-driven descendant, coalescing on TikTok and Tumblr around 2019–2022 in edit and vent communities. Subjects dissolve mid-frame to signal overwhelm or identity instability; editors trade presets and tutorials, and the style pairs with emo and hyperpop audio. Tools include pixel-sort scripts, VHS plugins and the famous Audacity-as-image-editor trick. Trajectory: glitchcore migrated into music visuals and fashion graphics, remaining a standard technique bank even as its tag culture cooled after 2022.",
    col: [{ h: "#00e5ff", n: "channel cyan" }, { h: "#ff00e5", n: "channel magenta" }, { h: "#0a0a0a", n: "static black" }, { h: "#39ff14", n: "signal green" }, { h: "#f5f5f5", n: "blown white" }],
