    n: "Metalheart", a: ["metal heart", "chrome y2k machine horror", "liquid chrome design"],
    cat: "Graphic Design", c: "chrome biomechanical graphic design", e: "internet_aesthetic", o: "Named from the Metalheart book series (Lindholm & Rönblom, early 2000s); codified by CARI",
    g: "Scandinavian-US design origin; global Y2K revival", p: "c. 1997–2004; revived 2021–present", era: "Y2K era and its revival",
    sum: "Liquid chrome, orthographic grids and machine-organic blobs: the darker mechanical wing of Y2K graphic design.",
    desc: "Metalheart names a strand of turn-of-the-millennium graphic design built from rendered liquid chrome, biomechanical blobs, orthographic machine grids and techno-tribal type — machine horror refined into sleek vector art. The term comes from the influential Metalheart book series by Andreas Lindholm and Anders F. Rönblom, which collected the scene's work; CARI codified it as a research category in its Y2K archaeology. Visually it sits between H.R. Giger's biomechanics and contemporary digital rendering: mercury drips, spiked chrome orbs, etched diagram lines and dot-matrix type on black or steel grounds. It flourished in Scandinavian studios, album covers, skateboards and Flash-era web experiments. Trajectory: fading after 2004, it was rediscovered in the 2020s Y2K revival through CARI write-ups and chrome-3D typography, feeding hyperpop covers and chrome posters.",
    col: [{ h: "#c9ccd1", n: "chrome silver" }, { h: "#2b2e33", n: "gunmetal" }, { h: "#e8e9eb", n: "mercury white" }, { h: "#1f5dff", n: "electric blue" }, { h: "#9fe870", n: "acid green" }],
