    n: "Skeuomorphism", a: ["skeuomorphic UI", "faux-real interface design", "iOS 6-era realism"],
    cat: "Graphic Design", c: "realistic-metaphor interface design", e: "internet_aesthetic", o: "Apple design practice (Aqua 2000; iOS 2007–2013), earlier industrial-design concept",
    g: "global consumer technology", p: "concept much older; UI peak 2007–2013", era: "glass-and-gloss smartphone era",
    sum: "Interfaces dressed as physical objects — stitched leather, brushed metal and glossy buttons — before flat design swept them away.",
    desc: "Skeuomorphism is the practice of designing digital interfaces to imitate physical materials and objects: Apple's early iPhone era wrapped apps in leather stitching, felt, brushed metal and linen, gave calendars torn page corners and buttons bulging gloss highlights — building on the Aqua interface's candy-gloss pinstripes introduced with Mac OS X in 2000. As an industrial-design term, skeuomorph names objects retaining decorative features of past technologies; in UI it served learnability, mapping screen actions to familiar things like legal pads and reel-to-reel recorders. Its consumer peak ran roughly 2007 to 2013, with iOS 6 as the style's densest statement. In 2013 Apple's iOS 7, under Jony Ive, replaced realism with flat design, fixing skeuomorphism as the designated predecessor. Debate since oscillates between mockery and revisionism; gloss revivals quote the vocabulary.",
    col: [{ h: "#b9bec4", n: "brushed silver" }, { h: "#8a5a33", n: "stitching tan" }, { h: "#d9d5cc", n: "linen grey" }, { h: "#2e5e3a", n: "casino felt green" }, { h: "#101418", n: "glossy black" }],
