    sum: "The sans-serif, shadow-less, tile-based interface language that replaced skeuomorphism across every screen.",
    desc: "Flat design is the industry-standard interface style built on solid colour fields, simple geometric shapes, generous whitespace and the removal of pseudo-3D ornament: no gloss, bevels or stitch textures. Its modern form crystallised with Microsoft's Metro design language in Windows 8 (2012), Apple's iOS 7 (2013) and Google's Material Design (2014), which added a codified minimal shadow system to restore hierarchy. Vocabulary: bright limited palettes, geometric sans-serif type, tiled grids, line icons and the long-shadow variant popular in 2013–2014. Usability researchers, notably the Nielsen Norman Group, criticised early flat UI for removing affordance cues, prompting flat-2.0 compromises that returned subtle depth. Community context: less a scene than the default visual environment of the 2010s–2020s web — its total dominance is what made retro-textured revivals read as refreshing.",
