    desc: "Neo-brutalist web design is a 2010s–2020s trend of websites that flaunt raw, unpolished construction: heavy black borders, harsh offset drop shadows, clashing flat colours, system or mono type, default-blue links and layouts that admit to being HTML. The name riffs on architectural Brutalism's honesty of material, transposed to markup; it grew from 2010s brutalist-websites directories and the indie-web backlash against corporate smoothness, going mainstream around 2020–2023 when Gumroad's rebrand and Figma templates spread the look. Vocabulary: unrounded corners, sticker-like badges, visible grids and intentionally ugly type pairings. Indie makers, portfolio sites and design students use it to signal honesty and anti-corporate wit. Trajectory: settled into a recognisable template language for indie products while continuing as a critique of flattened sameness.",
