    g: "indie-game and creative-coding community, global", p: "1980s roots; named 2021; active revival", era: "retro-computing revival",
    sum: "Black-and-white images built from ordered-dot dithering — the 1984 Macintosh look revived as a deliberate game-art style.",
    desc: "Ditherpunk is the name — popularised by developer Surma's 2021 essay Ditherpunk: The image I never forgot — for art made with 1-bit-style dithering: images rendered purely in black and white (or tiny palettes) whose tones emerge from ordered dot patterns, as on the 1984 Macintosh, early Game Boys and dot-matrix printers. Vocabulary: visible Bayer or Atkinson dither matrices, hard 1-bit silhouettes, chunky pixel grids and monochrome UI chrome. Community context: the style lives in indie games, demoscene work and creative-coding forums, where hobbyists write dithering shaders and treat constraints as aesthetics; Lucas Pope's Return of the Obra Dinn (2018) is its flagship, proving 1-bit rendering could carry a full commercial game. Trajectory: distinct from general pixel art — its constraint is colour depth, not resolution — it spreads through shader tutorials and handheld indie releases.",
