    g: "Northern European heartland; global scene", p: "1984–present", era: "home-computer era to present",
    sum: "Real-time rendered effects — copper bars, plasmas, twisting tunnels — made by coders competing for beauty under strict size limits.",
    desc: "Demoscene visuals are the moving-image style of the demoscene, the computer-art subculture producing demos: real-time audiovisual programs showing off programming skill. Born from 1980s software-cracking intros on the Commodore 64 and Amiga, the scene institutionalised through demo parties — Finland's Assembly, Germany's Revision — and archives like scene.org and pouet.net. Its canon is a catalogue of named effects: copper bars, plasma, rotozoomer, starfield, checkerboard floors and tunnel flights, synchronised to tracker music, with palettes exploiting hardware limits from 16-colour Amiga vividness to true-colour raymarching. Community context is competitive and craft-driven: compos, size-restricted intro categories and decades-old crews passing skills down. Trajectory: the scene fed the games industry; modern GPU demos keep the ethos — art as executable, beauty under constraint.",
