    g: "global text-network culture", p: "1960s–present", era: "pre-web and early-web text era",
    sum: "Pictures made entirely from typed characters — the original, and still living, vernacular of network screens.",
    desc: "ASCII art composes images from the 95 printable characters of ASCII, aligned in monospaced type: cats, roses, figlet block-letter banners, the obligatory smiley and sig-file creations. It predates the internet proper, emerging on teletypes and terminals in the 1960s–70s, then flourished on Usenet (groups like alt.ascii-art), email folklore and early BBSes — the only imagery plain text could carry. Vocabulary includes figlet banners, tonal shading built from characters like @#*.:- and box-drawing borders. Community context: distributed hobbyists traded collections through archives such as Chris.com's long-running library, and conventions like the rat and the rose became shared folklore. Trajectory: squeezed out by images on the web, ASCII art survived in code comments, terminal tools and FIGlet headers, and remains a living format in text-only art communities and terminal-UI revivals.",
    col: [{ h: "#ffffff", n: "terminal white" }, { h: "#33ff66", n: "phosphor green" }, { h: "#ffb000", n: "amber terminal" }, { h: "#000000", n: "screen black" }, { h: "#c8c8c8", n: "print-head grey" }],
