    desc: "ANSI art is the BBS-era extension of ASCII art: images built from the IBM PC's CP437 extended characters — half-blocks, shading blocks, box lines — with 16-colour control via ANSI.SYS escape codes. Because dial-up boards could display it natively, ANSI became the net's dominant pre-web art form: BBS login screens, menu frames, warez nfo files and elaborate group artpacks. Crews — most famously ACiD Productions — organised like graffiti artists, trading aliases and monthly packs; archives like 16colo.rs preserve tens of thousands of works. Vocabulary: the 80×25 canvas, checkerboard colour dithering, bold logo lettering and credit lines embedded in the art. Community context: rivalries, collaborations and drawn-by signatures made it a scene with its own economy. Trajectory: killed commercially by the graphical web, ANSI survives through revival crews, font projects and browser renderers.",
