    desc: "GeoCities homepage culture is the design language of the late-1990s amateur web: personal pages organised into themed neighbourhoods (SiliconValley for tech, Area51 for sci-fi), hand-coded and decorated with tiled starfield backgrounds, visitor counters, guestbooks, animated GIFs, autoplaying MIDIs and the era's totemic under-construction gif. GeoCities, founded in 1994, made home-page ownership mainstream; its closure by Yahoo in October 2009 triggered preservation efforts, including the Archive Team's torrent, studied in Lialina and Espenschied's One Terabyte of Kilobyte Age project. Vocabulary: clashing backgrounds, 216-colour-safe palettes, pixel fonts, award badges and best-viewed-in-Netscape notices; footer links to friends' pages prefigured social networks. Trajectory: the style anchors Webcore and Nostalgiacore imagery and drives the Neocities revival.",
