    desc: "This entry pairs the old web's two signature social-graph artefacts. Webrings — circular navigation loops linking sites on a topic, made practical by 1994–95 scripts — let small personal sites share audiences before search engines dominated; members displayed prev/random/next controls and ring logos. The 88×31 button is the era's micro-banner standard: an 88-by-31-pixel GIF crammed with 5–7px pixel type, used for made-with-Notepad tags, Any Browser badges, host credits, friend swaps and humour buttons. Both are documented in web-history writing; the badge's brutal compression bred a dense folk typography. Community context: reciprocal linking etiquette, no-hotlinking norms and button-wall curation expressed identity and alliance. Trajectory: killed by social share buttons, both were revived by the 2010s indie web — Neocities maintains webrings and button walls today.",
